#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: description
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Use Qiskit and IBM Quantum Compute Service developer tools to profile, "
"customize, and inspect your experiment settings and runtime environment."
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#, no-wrap
msgid "Tools to profile, customize, and inspect experiments"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<OpenInLabBanner notebookPath=\"tutorials/profiling-tools.ipynb\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*Usage estimate: 10 minutes on a Heron r2 processor (NOTE: This is an "
"estimate only. Your runtime might vary.)*\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To get good results from a quantum computer, you need more than a well-"
"designed circuit. The device's current condition, how your circuit is "
"scheduled, the noise that the circuit experiences, and when and how your job"
" runs on the quantum processing unit (QPU) all affect the quality of your "
"results. Qiskit and IBM Quantum&reg; Compute Service offer a growing set of "
"developer tools that expose this information. You can use these tools to act"
" on that information before you commit QPU time to a full experiment, and to"
" debug a finished experiment.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This tutorial uses a single end-to-end experiment to show how to combine "
"these tools at each stage of a typical development workflow. Not every tool "
"is useful for every experiment, so choose the tools that address the "
"questions that you have about your own workload.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The workflow is as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Prepare a mirror circuit of a Trotterized time evolution of a linear "
"transverse-field Ising model.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Select a backend with an alternative calibration data set that is designed "
"for error mitigation methods based on a noise model.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '3. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Run device benchmarking experiments so that the transpiler uses up-to-date "
"device properties when it selects a layout.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '4. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Inspect the timing information of the scheduled circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '5. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Learn Pauli-Lindblad and readout noise models for the circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '6. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Run noiseless, unmitigated, and mitigated local simulations. The noisy "
"simulations use the learned noise model to show how much improvement you "
"might gain from the selected error mitigation methods. This tutorial uses "
"probabilistic error cancellation (PEC) and Twirled Readout Error eXtinction "
"(TREX).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '7. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Get a usage estimate without running on a QPU, to check whether you need to "
"adjust the experiment to fit your budget.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '8. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Start a session and trigger calibration so that the experiment uses the most"
" recent calibration data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '9. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Run the experiment on the QPU by using Estimator, and compare the hardware "
"results with the simulated preview.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '10. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Inspect the execution spans to see whether there are significant gaps "
"between groups of executions (chunks).\n"
msgstr ""

