#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 1: Specify a circuit and observable {#step-1-specify-a-circuit-and-"
"observable}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Prepare a mirror circuit of a Trotterized time evolution of a linear "
"transverse-field Ising model. Mirror the circuit to cancel the circuit "
"unitary and form an identity circuit. Because the ideal output is the all-"
"zero state, the ideal expectation value of each single-qubit $Z$ observable "
"is 1. Mirroring also doubles the circuit depth, which amplifies the effect "
"of noise.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/profiling-"
"tools/extracted-outputs/caa280da-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 2: Optimize for quantum hardware execution {#step-2-optimize-for-"
"quantum-hardware-execution}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"2.1 Use alternative calibration if applicable {#21-use-alternative-"
"calibration-if-applicable}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"IBM&reg; calibrates its QPUs periodically for good performance across "
"general use cases. Some workloads, however, benefit from instructions whose "
"parameters are tuned differently. For more information, refer to [Specially "
"calibrated instructions](/guides/custom-calibration).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This tutorial uses an alternative calibration data set called "
"`slow_gates_ac`. This calibration contains a slower `cz` gate and modulates "
"the two-level system (TLS) bias to stabilize device errors over time. These "
"changes benefit error mitigation methods that rely on a learned noise model."
" The `slow_gates_ac` calibration is available only on Heron QPUs. If you run"
" on a Nighthawk QPU, use `slow_gates` instead.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"2.2 Run real-time device benchmarking {#22-run-real-time-device-"
"benchmarking}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"One of the most critical stages of transpilation is layout selection, which "
"maps virtual qubits in a circuit to physical qubits on the target device. A "
"good layout minimizes the number of SWAP operations needed and places the "
"circuit on the best-performing qubits, so the quality of the initial layout "
"can significantly affect the quality of your results.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Periodic benchmarking routines run on every IBM QPU to report the current "
"performance of its qubits: relaxation times ($T_1$), coherence times "
"($T_2$), readout errors, and one- and two-qubit gate errors. The Qiskit "
"transpiler uses these metrics during layout selection.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The properties of superconducting qubits, however, can drift on shorter "
"timescales, and transient defects, known as TLSs, can temporarily degrade a "
"qubit that performed well at calibration time. If the transpiler uses "
"outdated information, it cannot select the best layout. Therefore, it can be"
" worthwhile to spend some QPU time to characterize the device before you "
"select a layout.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Refer to the [Refresh backend properties with real-time benchmarking](/tutorials/refresh-backend-properties-with-real-time-benchmarking) tutorial for a detailed walk-through.\n"
"The characterization suite builds several experiments, submits multiple jobs, and fits the results. This process can take a few minutes in total. Enable INFO-level logging so that each stage prints its progress as it runs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`BackendCharacterization` is a convenience class that runs the "
"characterization experiments of your choice, analyzes the results, and "
"returns an updated `Backend` object that you can pass to the transpiler.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"You might see a warning that a backend was passed in as the mode while a "
"batch is open. This warning is expected, and you can safely ignore it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "2.3 Transpile to ISA {#23-transpile-to-isa}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Transform the circuit and observables to match the backend's instruction set"
" architecture (ISA) by using the refreshed device properties.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "2.4 Visualize circuit timing {#24-visualize-circuit-timing}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In addition to the instructions in a circuit, you might want to visualize "
"the circuit's schedule, to find idle time on qubits or inefficient "
"instruction alignment. You can get the most detailed and accurate timing "
"information from IBM Quantum Compute Service by using dry-run mode.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The circuit in this tutorial is static, so its schedule is not very "
"informative. You can get similar timing information locally from the circuit"
" and the backend properties, for example by using the [timeline "
"drawer](/guides/visualize-circuit-timing) built into Qiskit. This tutorial "
"includes this step to show how to request and visualize the timing "
"information. Timing information from the service is most useful for [dynamic"
" circuits](/guides/classical-feedforward-and-control-flow). Dynamic circuits"
" contain mid-circuit measurements, conditional operations, and feedforward, "
"which add implicit operations such as broadcasting measurement results and "
"determining branches. The timing of these operations cannot be determined "
"from the circuit and backend properties alone.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Refer to [Visualize circuit timing](/guides/qiskit-runtime-circuit-timing) for more information.\n"
"Scheduled circuit timing is available only for Sampler and Executor jobs, so the following cell submits the circuit in dry-run mode by using Sampler. Jobs that you submit in dry-run mode do not run on real QPUs and are not charged.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This figure is too large to display in a notebook. The following screenshot "
"shows a summarized view.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"View a screenshot of the scheduled circuit timing\">\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Scheduled circuit timing for the mirror circuit, showing each qubit "
"channel over time](/docs/images/tutorials/profiling-tools/scheduler-"
"timing.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 3: Apply error mitigation and test locally {#step-3-apply-error-"
"mitigation-and-test-locally}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "3.1 Cliffordize the circuit {#31-cliffordize-the-circuit}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The cost of general noisy simulation grows exponentially with the number of "
"qubits, and most laptops cannot simulate more than about 32 qubits. "
"Therefore, first convert the circuit to a Clifford circuit, which a "
"stabilizer simulator can handle far more efficiently. The conversion rounds "
"each rotation angle to a Clifford angle, so the simulated circuit "
"approximates the original circuit rather than reproducing it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "3.2 Apply PEC with TREX {#32-apply-pec-with-trex}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"It can be difficult to select error mitigation methods that balance cost and"
" accuracy for your specific experiment. You can use the `select-error-"
"mitigation` AI agent skill to analyze the available methods for your "
"workload. The skill presents a summary report that explains whether each "
"method was selected and why.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Refer to the [Qiskit AI skills](https://github.com/Qiskit/skills) GitHub repository for more information.\n"
"This tutorial uses PEC with TREX directly, so that you can complete it without an AI agent.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"PEC is a technique that mitigates quantum noise when you calculate the "
"expectation values of observables. Under ideal conditions, PEC can produce "
"an unbiased estimate of the expectation values. It learns the noise of each "
"layer of two-qubit gates, then runs an ensemble of circuits that, on "
"average, apply the inverse of that noise. PEC uses Pauli twirling to tailor "
"the noise into a Pauli channel, which can be learned and inverted "
"efficiently.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In practice, PEC is only as accurate as the learned noise model. The sparse "
"Pauli-Lindblad model includes only one- and two-qubit error terms on "
"neighboring qubits, and it cannot fully capture effects such as crosstalk "
"between distant qubits or time-correlated errors. The device noise can also "
"drift between noise learning and execution. PEC does not cancel noise that "
"the model misses, and it overcompensates for noise that the model "
"overestimates. Therefore, mitigated results can still deviate from the ideal"
" values in either direction.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"TREX is a technique that mitigates measurement errors, and is useful when you estimate the expectation values of Pauli observables. It relies on measurement twirling, which diagonalizes the readout-error transfer matrix and makes it easy to invert. TREX learns a rescaling term from the diagonal noise channel by measuring twirled calibration circuits that are initialized in the zero state.\n"
"You can enable both PEC and TREX by setting Estimator options. This tutorial uses the client-side implementations of the Qiskit SamplerV2 and EstimatorV2 interfaces, which were introduced in `qiskit-ibm-runtime` v0.50.0. These client-side primitives perform most of the pre- and post-processing locally and delegate execution to the Executor primitive. When the primitives run on the client side, you get greater transparency, faster feedback when you debug, and finer control.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The client-side primitives implement the same API as the legacy server-side primitives. Therefore, you can usually switch to the client-side primitives by changing only the import statement.\n"
"Enable INFO-level logging to see the progress of client-side pre- and post-processing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"3.3 Learn noise models and run the Cliffordized circuit {#33-learn-noise-"
"models-and-run-the-cliffordized-circuit}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Both PEC and TREX require noise learning, which you can do with "
"`NoiseLearnerV3`. The steps are as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Find the unique two-qubit gate layers and measurement layers in the circuit,"
" for the Pauli-Lindblad and TREX protocols, respectively.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Pass the layers to `NoiseLearnerV3` to learn the noise models.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '3. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Convert the learned noise models to Pauli-Lindblad maps.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Estimator does not use a pre-learned TREX noise model. Instead, it performs "
"its own TREX learning. The following code learns both models anyway, because"
" the noisy simulation uses the learned readout model.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Refer to [Noise learning helper](/guides/noise-learning) for more information about `NoiseLearnerV3`.\n"
"In addition to the noise-learning experiment, run the Cliffordized circuit on the QPU. This step is not always necessary, but it provides a comparison for the simulated results in Step 3.4.4. Run both in a batch to minimize how much the noise profile drifts between the two.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "3.4 Perform local simulation {#34-perform-local-simulation}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"With `qiskit-ibm-runtime` local mode, you can perform both noiseless and "
"noisy simulations locally with minimal code changes. Use local mode to "
"verify syntactic correctness, compare the effects of different mitigation "
"methods, and check how well the learned noise model agrees with hardware "
"results.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Local mode uses largely the same interface as QPU execution. In most cases, "
"you need to change only the backend that you pass when you initialize the "
"primitive, and then rerun the same code. Each of the following subsections "
"creates its own `Estimator` instance, so you can run them without changing a"
" previous cell.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Refer to [Local mode](/guides/local-testing-mode) for more information.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "3.4.1 Run a noiseless simulation {#341-run-a-noiseless-simulation}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"3.4.2 Run a noisy simulation with the learned Pauli-Lindblad model "
"{#342-run-a-noisy-simulation-with-the-learned-pauli-lindblad-model}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition type=\"caution\">\n"
"Do not use a fake backend or a Qiskit Aer noise model if you plan to use the learned Pauli-Lindblad noise model in your local simulation. If you do, the simulation applies the noise twice.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"3.4.3 Run a noisy simulation with PEC and TREX {#343-run-a-noisy-simulation-"
"with-pec-and-trex}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"3.4.4 Compare the simulation results {#344-compare-the-simulation-results}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{/* cspell:ignore frameon sharey */}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Plot the expectation value of each single-qubit $Z$ observable from the "
"three simulations, together with the unmitigated QPU result for the same "
"Clifford circuit from Step 3.3. Because the circuit is a mirror circuit, "
"every ideal value is 1. The error bars show the standard error that "
"Estimator reports for each value.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The closer the unmitigated simulation is to the unmitigated QPU result, the "
"better the learned noise model describes the hardware. Use this comparison "
"as a check before you spend QPU time on the mitigated experiment. If the two"
" results disagree significantly, PEC is also unlikely to get close to the "
"ideal values on hardware. In that case, consider relearning the noise model "
"or selecting a different layout. You can also relearn the noise model with "
"different `NoiseLearnerV3` options, such as more randomizations or "
"additional layer-pair depths, and check whether the agreement improves. For "
"example, in Step 3.3 this tutorial sets `num_randomizations` to 64 instead "
"of the default value of 32, because the additional randomizations improved "
"the agreement for this circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`NoiseLearnerV3` runs a standard learning protocol. For full control over "
"the learning experiment, you can build your own with [Qiskit noise "
"learning](/addons/qiskit-noise-learning/guides/workflow).\n"
msgstr ""

