#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "3.5 Estimate usage {#35-estimate-usage}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The main purpose of dry-run mode is to estimate usage. Unlike local mode, "
"dry-run mode submits the job to IBM Quantum Compute Service, which returns "
"an accurate usage estimate but **random result data**. Dry-run jobs do not "
"run on real QPUs and do not consume any QPU time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Dry-run jobs are not available on the Open or Pay-As-You-Go plans.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Refer to [Dry-run jobs](/guides/dry-run-jobs) for more information.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Step 4: Run on hardware {#step-4-run-on-hardware}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"4.1 Initialize a session and run calibration {#41-initialize-a-session-and-"
"run-calibration}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"QPU calibration optimizes many parameters that define the electrical signals"
" that drive the quantum gates and operations. Calibration jobs run "
"throughout the day between user jobs, but not during an active session. "
"These parameters can drift within a short period of time. For the best "
"possible results, consider triggering calibration at the start of a session,"
" or periodically during a long-running session.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Currently, only readout calibration is supported, and it takes approximately"
" five minutes of wall-clock time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"4.2 Run on hardware with Estimator {#42-run-on-hardware-with-estimator}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The following cells run three jobs in the batch or session:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A noise-learning experiment to refresh the noise model. This experiment is "
"useful if you expect the noise model from Step 3.3 to be outdated when the "
"Estimator job runs. In a batch, the Estimator jobs can run much later than "
"the noise-learning job, because jobs from other users can run on the QPU "
"between them. A session gives your workload exclusive access to the QPU, so "
"the noise model is more likely to still be accurate when the experiment "
"runs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The experiment with mitigation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The experiment without mitigation, for comparison.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Plot the mitigated and unmitigated hardware results together with the ideal "
"values. Because the circuit is a mirror circuit, every ideal value is 1, so "
"the distance between each point and the dashed line shows the remaining "
"error. Any remaining deviation from the ideal value, whether above or below "
"it, reflects the statistical uncertainty of PEC and the limits of the "
"learned noise model described in Step 3.2.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To further improve the results, you can try the following:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Increase the number of shots or randomizations to reduce the statistical "
"uncertainty of the mitigated estimates. The cost grows with the PEC sampling"
" overhead.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Relearn the noise model immediately before the experiment, in the same "
"session, to reduce the effect of noise drift.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Learn the noise model more precisely by increasing the `num_randomizations`,"
" `shots_per_randomization`, or `layer_pair_depths` options of "
"`NoiseLearnerV3`. Errors in the learned rates bias the mitigated results, "
"and additional PEC shots do not remove this bias.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Add [non-Markovian error checks](/addons/qiskit-"
"mitigation/guides/postselection-with-non-markovian-error-checks) to detect "
"shots that are likely corrupted by non-Markovian errors, which the learned "
"noise model does not describe. Then discard those shots by postselection. "
"Apply the same checks when you learn the noise model, by using the "
"`bit_flip_checks` options of `NoiseLearnerV3`. Otherwise, the learned model "
"includes errors that postselection removes from the experiment, and PEC "
"overcompensates for them. Stricter postselection discards more shots, so "
"balance the accuracy gain against the number of shots that you keep.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/profiling-"
"tools/extracted-outputs/3cd8d65c-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"4.3 Compare hardware and simulation results {#43-compare-hardware-and-"
"simulation-results}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In Step 3.4, you used local simulation to preview how much PEC and TREX "
"might improve the results. Compare that preview with the QPU results. If the"
" simulated improvement and the hardware improvement are similar, local "
"simulation is a useful way to evaluate mitigation settings for this workload"
" before you spend QPU time on them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The comparison is approximate, for two reasons:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The simulations run the Cliffordized circuit from Step 3.1, while the "
"hardware jobs run the original circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The simulations use the noise model learned in Step 3.3, while the mitigated"
" hardware job uses the noise model that was refreshed in Step 4.2.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For a direct check of the noise model itself, use the comparison of the "
"Clifford circuit results in Step 3.4.4.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/profiling-"
"tools/extracted-outputs/3b67439e-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In this run, mitigation reduced the mean absolute deviation from 0.290 to "
"0.046 in the simulation (about a factor of six) and from 0.202 to 0.050 on "
"the QPU (about a factor of four). In both cases, the mitigated values "
"scatter around 1, and most of them agree with 1 within their error bars.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "4.4 Inspect execution spans {#44-inspect-execution-spans}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"When you submit circuits with multiple configurations, such as with gate "
"twirling, IBM Quantum Compute Service does not necessarily run all circuit "
"configurations at the same time. Instead, it groups them into chunks and "
"runs one chunk per shot loop. A shot loop is a single round of execution on "
"the quantum hardware, in which every circuit in the chunk is run for the "
"requested number of shots.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"All jobs that are submitted to a real QPU require some classical pre-"
"processing, such as translation into pulses that can be sent to the "
"hardware. Because this pre-processing can be done in parallel, the service "
"dequeues and starts multiple jobs at a time. The QPU, however, can process "
"only one unit of work at a time. Therefore, if your job generates multiple "
"chunks, they might be interleaved with the chunks of other users' jobs, and "
"each chunk might have to wait for its turn on the QPU.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The job result metadata reports the QPU execution timing for each chunk. The"
" following visualization shows whether there are significant gaps between "
"the executions of the chunks in your job. If the gaps are wider than your "
"experiment can tolerate, you might want to rerun the job, or use [session "
"execution mode](/guides/execution-modes#session-mode), which gives your "
"workload exclusive access to the QPU.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Refer to [View execution spans](/guides/sampler-input-output#view-execution-"
"spans) for more information.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Chunk execution timeline for the mitigated job, showing two chunks and the"
" gap between them](/docs/images/tutorials/profiling-tools/chunk-"
"timing.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

